' Transport — synchronous roUrlTransfer wrapper.
'
' Transport is the only store that touches Roku's networking primitives. Real
' requests go through a blocking HTTP client (roUrlTransfer async calls parked
' on a message port with Wait); tests inject a fake client so every store,
' Transport itself included, runs in the brs interpreter with no device calls.
'
' The client seam returns a raw result { ok, status, body, error }. Transport
' normalizes that into { ok, status, json, error }: it parses the body as JSON
' and treats an unparseable body on an otherwise-successful response as a
' failure. Stores read result.json and never care where the bytes came from.
'
' Helpers live as instance methods: Roku scopes user-defined global functions
' per component, so a bare global called from a method that is invoked from
' another component's code resolves to invalid (call of a non-function). Only
' the platform's own globals (CreateObject, Wait, ParseJson, ...) resolve from
' any scope; anything user-defined must be reachable through m.

const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 15000

' The dedicated-request timeout ceiling for the one caller that can legitimately
' take tens of seconds (a full addon-collection sync against a slow account). A
' default-sized request would abort it partway, so it rides the long path.
const LONG_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 120000

' The real client. Serializes a body assocarray to JSON and issues a blocking
' GET or POST over roUrlTransfer. The transfer runs async and Wait parks this
' thread until its roUrlEvent lands: the synchronous GetToString/PostFromString
' entry points discard the response code (and roUrlTransfer no longer exposes
' GetResponseCode), while the async path surfaces both the body
' (roUrlEvent.GetString) and the status (roUrlEvent.GetResponseCode).
function CreateSyncHttpClient() as object
    client = {}

    ' Shared roUrlTransfer round-trip for both timeouts. roUrlTransfer is not
    ' re-entrant, so each request builds its own transfer + port; the two
    ' request flavors only differ in how long Wait parks before cancelling.
    ' Lives on the client AA (not as a bare global) because user-defined globals
    ' resolve to invalid when a caller from another component invokes the
    ' request functions — anything user-defined must be reachable through m.
    client._execute = function(request as object) as object
        result = { ok: false, status: 0, body: "", error: "" }
        transfer = CreateObject("roUrlTransfer")
        transfer.SetUrl(request.url)
        if request.headers <> invalid then transfer.SetHeaders(request.headers)
        transfer.RetainBodyOnError(true)
        port = CreateObject("roMessagePort")
        transfer.SetMessagePort(port)

        issued = false
        if request.method = "POST"
            payload = ""
            if request.body <> invalid then payload = FormatJson(request.body)
            issued = transfer.AsyncPostFromString(payload)
        else
            issued = transfer.AsyncGetToString()
        end if
        if not issued
            result.error = "request could not be issued"
            return result
        end if

        event = Wait(request.timeoutMs, port)
        if event = invalid
            transfer.AsyncCancel()
            result.error = "request timed out"
            return result
        end if
        if Type(event) <> "roUrlEvent"
            result.error = "unexpected port message"
            return result
        end if

        result.body = event.GetString()
        result.status = event.GetResponseCode()
        result.ok = result.status >= 200 and result.status < 300
        if not result.ok
            if result.status < 0
                reason = event.GetFailureReason()
                if reason = invalid or reason = "" then reason = "transfer failed"
                result.error = reason
            else
                result.error = "HTTP " + result.status.ToStr()
            end if
        end if
        return result
    end function

    client.request = function(method as string, url as string, headers = invalid as dynamic, body = invalid as dynamic, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS as integer) as object
        return m._execute({
            method: method
            url: url
            headers: headers
            body: body
            timeoutMs: timeoutMs
        })
    end function
    client.requestLong = function(method as string, url as string, headers = invalid as dynamic, body = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m._execute({
            method: method
            url: url
            headers: headers
            body: body
            timeoutMs: LONG_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS
        })
    end function
    return client
end function

' Percent-encode a value for a URL path segment or query component. The
' platform owns the real encoder: roUrlTransfer.Escape percent-encodes per
' RFC 3986, including proper UTF-8 for accented characters, and every caller
' runs inside a Task node where roUrlTransfer is available. The byte loop is
' only the fallback for environments without the component (the brs interpreter
' in `npm test`), where ASCII values encode identically.
'
' Named PercentEncode while the Transport method below is EncodeQueryValue, so
' the two never collide: one name for both would make the unqualified call
' inside the method resolve back to the method. The method is deliberately not
' UrlEncode either — that is the deprecated roUrlTransfer method, this already
' calls its replacement, and a method of that name reads as a call to it.
'
' File-scope with that method forwarding to it, deliberately: stores must reach
' user-defined helpers through m, but the scripted test transport is a plain
' object and has no m.transport to call. Keeping the implementation here lets
' that mock forward to THIS function rather than carry a second copy that could
' drift from it and make the tests assert an encoding the device never
' produces.
function PercentEncode(value as string) as string
    try
        transfer = CreateObject("roUrlTransfer")
        return transfer.Escape(value)
    catch e
    end try

    result = ""
    HEX = "0123456789ABCDEF"
    i = 1
    while i <= Len(value)
        b = Asc(Mid(value, i, 1))
        if (b >= 65 and b <= 90) or (b >= 97 and b <= 122) or (b >= 48 and b <= 57) or b = 45 or b = 95 or b = 46 or b = 126
            result = result + Chr(b)
        else
            result = result + "%" + Mid(HEX, b \ 16 + 1, 1) + Mid(HEX, (b mod 16) + 1, 1)
        end if
        i = i + 1
    end while
    return result
end function

class Transport
    client as object

    sub new(client = invalid as dynamic)
        if client = invalid then client = CreateSyncHttpClient()
        m.client = client
    end sub

    ' Percent-encode a value destined for a URL. Stores that assemble a URL call
    ' this through m rather than reaching for the file-scope function directly,
    ' because a bare user-defined global is only resolvable within the component
    ' that declares it.
    '
    ' Renamed from UrlEncode, which was never a call to that method: the encoder
    ' here has always been roUrlTransfer.Escape. The name was the only thing
    ' wrong with it, and it is a name a static analyser reads as the deprecated
    ' roUrlTransfer.UrlEncode — a deprecation this file does not have, reported
    ' against a call nobody can find.
    function EncodeQueryValue(value as string) as string
        return PercentEncode(value)
    end function

    function Get(url as string, headers = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m.Result(m.client.request("GET", url, headers, invalid))
    end function

    ' A GET whose body comes back as text instead of being parsed as JSON.
    '
    ' Only for the streaming server's HLS manifest, which is not JSON. Through
    ' Get a perfectly good playlist reads back as "invalid JSON response" — the
    ' one response whose text is the entire diagnosis would be the one response
    ' thrown away.
    ' timeoutMs is a parameter because the one caller that polls — the playlist
    ' readiness probe — wants a short one, and at the default 15s a dead server
    ' would let its six attempts stretch the player's wait past a minute and a
    ' half. The default keeps every other caller's behaviour untouched.
    function GetRaw(url as string, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS as integer) as object
        return m.client.request("GET", url, invalid, invalid, timeoutMs)
    end function

    function Post(url as string, body = invalid as dynamic, headers = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m.Result(m.client.request("POST", url, headers, body))
    end function

    ' Long-window POST for requests that may legitimately run tens of seconds —
    ' today the Stremio API's addon-collection sync, which can sit behind a slow
    ' account. Uses requestLong so the default timeout is never stretched for
    ' every caller.
    function PostLong(url as string, body = invalid as dynamic, headers = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m.Result(m.client.requestLong("POST", url, headers, body))
    end function

    ' Build a full add-on endpoint URL from a stored address that may carry a
    ' query string (the user's add-on config, e.g. a ?token=... API key).
    ' The endpoint path is inserted BEFORE the query so the request hits the
    ' right resource with the user's args intact.
    function EndpointUrl(storedAddress as string, endpointPath as string) as string
        at = storedAddress.InStr("?")
        if at < 0 then return storedAddress + endpointPath
        return storedAddress.Left(at) + endpointPath + storedAddress.Mid(at)
    end function

    ' Normalize a raw client result into the transport result: parse JSON when
    ' the body is non-empty, and demote a 2xx with a broken body to a failure.
    function Result(raw as object) as object
        result = {
            ok: raw.ok
            status: raw.status
            json: invalid
            error: raw.error
        }
        body = raw.body
        if body <> invalid and body <> ""
            parsed = m.Parse(body)
            if parsed = invalid
                result.ok = false
                result.error = "invalid JSON response"
            end if
            result.json = parsed
        end if
        return result
    end function

    ' Parse is a guard around ParseJson: the Roku runtime throws on malformed
    ' input rather than returning invalid, and Transport must turn that into a
    ' clean failure instead of a crash.
    function Parse(body as string) as dynamic
        try
            return ParseJson(body)
        catch e
            return invalid
        end try
    end function
end class